INT. DEAD MAN'S TACKLE SHOP - BACK ROOM - DAY

Rods in racks. Tins of lures. A calendar from 2009. Dust motes in a shaft of window light.

GLORIA ESPINOSA-WRAY (60s, widow's black, silver bun) sits on an upturned bucket, a shoebox in her lap. Her son RAUL (30s, work shirt, wedding ring turned the wrong way) stands at the workbench, sorting reels into a crate.

Neither speaks.

He lifts a reel. Examines it. Sets it down.

RAUL
This one's good.

GLORIA
Your father cursed that one.

RAUL
He cursed all of them.

GLORIA
He cursed them gently.

Raul almost laughs. He doesn't.

RAUL
I can have the shop listed by Friday.

GLORIA
Do what you want.

RAUL
It's your shop.

GLORIA
It was his shop.

RAUL
Ma.

GLORIA
(opening the shoebox)
You didn't come.

RAUL
I came to the funeral.

GLORIA
You didn't come in March.

RAUL
I was working.

GLORIA
He asked.

RAUL
I know he asked.

GLORIA
He asked every day.

RAUL
And I said I'd come.

GLORIA
And you said you'd come.

A drip from the ceiling. A bucket catches it. Gloria lifts a photograph from the box. A boy on a pier, grinning with a fish too big for him.

GLORIA (CONT'D)
Look.

He doesn't.

RAUL
I don't want to.

GLORIA
You were eight.

RAUL
I remember.

GLORIA
He kept it in the register. Under the tray.

RAUL
That's stupid.

GLORIA
It's what he did.

RAUL
He'd have let me know. If it mattered.

GLORIA
He did let you know. Every day. By asking.

Raul grips the bench. The knuckles go white. A reel falls from the crate and rolls across the floor.

RAUL
You think I don't know that?
GLORIA
^You think I don't know what you think?

Their voices overlap and collide. They stop. Gloria sets down the photograph.

GLORIA (CONT'D)
Sit.

RAUL
I'm fine.

GLORIA
Sit.

He sits on a crate. Hands between his knees.

RAUL
I was scared.

GLORIA
Of what?

RAUL
Of seeing him small.

GLORIA
He wasn't small.

RAUL
He was. Ma. In the photos you sent, he was.

GLORIA
He was still your father.

RAUL
That's the thing.

GLORIA
What?

RAUL
I wanted him to stay the one with the fish.

GLORIA
(very quietly)
Oh, Raul.

He bends forward, forehead on his fists. Gloria sets the box aside, crosses the room in her slow black shoes, and rests a hand on the back of his neck, the way you steady a young horse. The drip falls into the bucket, steady as a clock.

RAUL
I should've come.

GLORIA
Yes.

RAUL
That's it? Yes?

GLORIA
You want me to say it was fine?

RAUL
I want you to say something.

GLORIA
Help me pack the rods.

He wipes his face on his sleeve. Stands. Takes one end of the long rod rack. She takes the other. They carry it out into the light, not looking at each other, the way people do who have finally said enough.
